KEY DECISIONS

- Tensor-first fabric ordering convention: when a multi-fabric DSP
  has a clear "primary" path (tensor for ML inference, MMA for
  ADAS, HMX for matrix), put it as fabric[0] in the YAML. Loader
  picks fabric[0] for chip-level compute_units / energy_per_flop_fp32
  surfaces. CEVA NPM11 demonstrated zero drift; SA8775P/QRB5165/
  QCS6490 followed the same pattern. Synopsys EV7x and TI TDA4
  family mirrored hand-coded ordering (VPU/C7x first), accepting
  3-5 documented drifts that collapse post-cleanup.

- BOM overlay pattern: BOMCostProfile is NOT in *Block YAMLs today
  (none of the existing v9 SKU YAMLs carry it). For SKUs with
  commercial cost data (SA8775P, QRB5165, QCS6490), the cleanup
  wrapper attaches BOM post-load. Establishes precedent for any
  future SKU with cost data not captured in the schema. v11+
  candidate: add bom_cost_profile to Market block in the YAML
  schema and move overlays inside the YAMLs.

- Inheritance vs alias for v10 OnDieFabric: each block kind has
  its own topology enum (CPU's infinity_fabric, DPU's aie_mesh,
  NPU's systolic). Inheritance preserves per-kind enum validation
  while collapsing shared fields. Alias would have forced
  unifying all 6 topology enums (data loss).

- Endpoint-count naming reconciliation: CPU's stop_count and GPU's
  controller_count renamed to unit_count (matching 4-of-6).
  Per-arch field description on the base class documents what
  "unit" means in each context ("ring stop" for CPU, "memory
  controller" for GPU).

- Catalog count test loosening: per-SKU PR's `== N` assertions
  loosened to `>= N` (subset semantics) following the established
  pattern. Avoids regression when future SKUs land.


